	abstract = {Summary This chapter explores the use of doorstep concerns data in a longitudinal survey setting to understand the relationships between different nonresponse error components under the total survey error (TSE) framework. Taking advantage of data from four waves of the Consumer Expenditure Interview Survey (CE), the chapter tests the response continuum model by examining the relationship between a person's reluctance and their likelihood to participate in the CE. Using Wave 2 expenditure data, the chapter empirically examines total nonresponse bias in later waves of CE and the relative contributions of bias due to unit nonresponse and bias due to item nonresponse under the TSE framework. The chapter also explores the use of doorstep concerns data in nonresponse adjustment and examines the changes in later waves' estimates of various expenditures before and after including doorstep concerns in the nonresponse adjustments.},
