	isbn = {978-1-119-04170-2},
	url = {https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/9781119041702.ch12},
	abstract = {Summary This chapter reviews some recent developments in computer-assisted recorded interviewing (CARI), mobile technology, geographic information systems (GIS), and field management informed by the total survey error (TSE) paradigm. Survey operations are widely understood to include data collection activities, though the scope of this can be ambiguous in some modes, such as the web. In the survey life cycle, survey operations can be defined as everything that happens on a survey between design and final report creation or data delivery. The chapter describes the CARI coding process at Westat and discusses the results of a recent experiment that demonstrates how providing feedback to interviewers may help to improve the quality of survey interviews. It presents an approach to integrating paradata and quality metrics for continuous improvement, and suggests that TSE concepts can be effective when applied during survey operations by supervisors who have direct responsibility for the data collection activities.},
