	abstract = {Summary This chapter defines mixed-mode surveys as surveys that use multiple modes of data collection. It explores different methods for isolating the measurement effects associated with different methods of data collection. Some surveys use a sequence of modes of data collection in an effort to reduce cost. For example, the American Community Survey (ACS), the very large repeated cross-sectional survey that is used to update U.S. population estimates between censuses, has used multiple modes of data collection from its inception. The chapter examines evidence that giving respondents a choice of modes for responding may actually backfire, reducing the ultimate response rates. It discusses how to get substantial numbers of respondents to complete surveys online and also examines whether the order in which modes are offered affects the final response rate. Cross-sectional surveys often start with the lowest cost method of data collection and switch to more expensive modes for nonresponse follow-up.},
